---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";

/**
 * The page a sceptical reader actually opens. Everything on it is checkable
 * against an external register, which is the point — the legal facts here are
 * duplicated in toohardbasket.org.au's src/lib/about.ts, and the two must not
 * be allowed to drift.
 */
const legalStatus = [
  ["Registered name", "Australian Public Interest Alliance Incorporated"],
  ["Registration number", "IARN A1046494Z"],
  ["Incorporated", "26 November 2025, Western Australia"],
  ["Legislation", "Associations Incorporation Act 2015 (WA)"],
  ["ABN", "33 842 796 620"],
  ["Rules", "The Model Rules for incorporated associations (WA)"],
  ["Financial year", "Ends 30 June"],
];
---

<Base
  title="Governance"
  description="APIA's legal status, who is accountable for what it publishes, how it is funded, and how corrections are handled."
>
  <section class="wrap measure">
    <p class="eyebrow">Governance</p>
    <h1>Who we are and who answers for this</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      If you are checking whether data published here can be relied on, this is the page
      you want.
    </p>

    <h2>Legal status</h2>
    <dl class="facts">
      {legalStatus.map(([k, v]) => (
        <div><dt>{k}</dt><dd>{v}</dd></div>
      ))}
    </dl>
    <p>
      APIA is not registered with the ACNC and is not endorsed as a deductible gift
      recipient. Donations to APIA are not tax deductible.
    </p>

    <h2 id="accountable">Who is accountable</h2>
    <p>
      Alan Hewitt, President, is responsible for the data APIA publishes and answers for
      any error in it.
    </p>
    <p>
      APIA is governed by a committee of three members under the Model Rules. The full
      committee is recorded on the public register maintained by Consumer Protection (WA),
      which anyone may search.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>In practice this is one person’s work.</strong> The other two committee
      members provide the governance an incorporated association requires; they do not
      direct APIA’s projects or review them day to day. That is worth saying plainly,
      because “a committee of three” implies more institutional weight than exists, and a
      reader who worked it out later would be entitled to wonder what else had been
      oversold.
    </p>
    <p>
      It also matters less than it might. Nothing APIA publishes asks you to trust the
      organisation. Every figure is computed by published code from published data, every
      row links to the official record it came from, and the method is published alongside
      it — including what it cannot see. If something is wrong, it is wrong in a way you
      can demonstrate, which is a better guarantee than a larger committee would be.
    </p>

    <h2>Funding</h2>
    <p>
      APIA is self-funded by its founder and has received no external funding to date.
    </p>
    <p>
      If that changes, this page will say so and name the source. We would rather tell you
      than have you find out.
    </p>

    <h2 id="corrections">Corrections</h2>
    <p>
      Every dataset APIA publishes carries its sources and its method, so you can check our
      working.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you find an error, write to
      {" "}<a href="mailto:info@apia.org.au?subject=correction">info@apia.org.au</a> with
      “correction” in the subject line. We will check it, fix it if we are wrong, and record
      what changed — so corrections are visible rather than silent.
    </p>

    <h2>Conflicts of interest</h2>
    <p>
      APIA operates under the Model Rules, which govern the circumstances in which any
      payment may be made to a committee member. Committee members declare personal
      interests in matters before the committee, and any such payment is approved by the
      committee and recorded in the minutes.
    </p>
    <p>
      One standing disclosure is set out in full on the
      {" "}<a href="/about/#disclosure">About page</a>.
    </p>
  </section>
</Base>
